nodecode-ros is a rewrite of arcabotai's arcabotai/hermes-ros:

  https://github.com/arcabotai/hermes-ros
  commit 57a67b89142a6b7659a2b40c4796f8e1cad5e542
  MIT License, Copyright (c) 2026 Arca Computer, Inc. (LICENSE)

The rewrite is the plugin's robot link, in Common Lisp against the Nodecode
cell contract: the same nine verbs (list, echo, drive, publish, call a
service, read the pose, take a camera snapshot, stop, report the safety
state), the same two backends -- the built-in 2D mock robot, which needs no
ROS at all, and a real robot or simulator behind a rosbridge websocket -- and
the same safety interlock: a linear and angular speed ceiling, a bounded
duration, an optional rectangular geofence checked on the projected path, a
write allowlist for topics and services by glob, a latching e-stop that only a
human clears, and a bounded audit of every blocked call. The plugin enforced
that from a pre_tool_call hook because it did not own its tools; a cell does,
so the same checks run inside the tools that write, and ros_stop is never
blocked -- stopping always works. The mock's unicycle, its cones, its walls,
its expiring velocity command and its three services are kept, as is the
camera snapshot written under this home with the MEDIA: line that shows it to
the operator, and the rosbridge protocol the plugin's own small client spoke
(call_service, subscribe, advertise/publish), here over the image's
websocket-driver instead of a Python dependency.

What is left out, and why: the plugin's Hermes dashboard tab and its
read-only web API, because that is Hermes' web UI and a Nodecode cell has its
own page and `/ros`; the separate plugin data directory with estop.json,
blocked.jsonl and last_pose.json, because the same facts are now the cell's
own files under this home (the latch) and its ring of blocked calls in the
process; the image-decoding helper module, because a rosbridge CompressedImage
is written out byte for byte and a raw Image is re-encoded with the image's
own PNG writer; and the bundled skill prompt, because the same guidance is
the manual (help :ros) that every request carries a line of. The docker demo,
the fake camera publisher and the turtlesim verification scripts are
development scaffolding, not part of what a user runs.

Nothing of the original's Python is bundled. The cell's own code is Common
Lisp, it opens one websocket when a robot is configured, and with the default
mock backend it touches nothing outside this home.
